.TH BORG-BACKUP 8 "2026-09-13" "borg-backup" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
borg-backup \- setup and day-2 operations for a local + external borg backup system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B borg-backup
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.RB [ --dry-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B borg-backup
manages two
.BR borg (1)
repokey-encrypted repositories, each driven by root
.BR systemd (1)
units:
.IP \(bu 4
.B local
\- an on-disk repo, backed up on a timer
.IP \(bu 4
.B external
\- a USB repo, backed up via a
.BR udev (7)
rule on plug-in
.PP
All host-specific configuration lives in
.IR /etc/borg/borg-backup.env ,
written by
.B setup
and sourced by every other command and by the backup/check scripts
themselves.
.PP
Passing
.B --dry-run
anywhere on the command line makes every command print what it would run
or write, instead of running or writing it. No files or devices are
touched.
.B setup
still performs its read-only discovery for real under
.BR --dry-run ,
so its prompts show real data (block devices, existing config, etc).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Interactive wizard. Asks what to back up, where the local repo lives,
whether to configure an external drive (lists block devices, requires
retyping the device path before wiping it), tuning parameters, and a
passphrase. Writes
.IR /etc/borg/borg-backup.env ,
installs the CLI, scripts, and systemd units, optionally partitions and
formats the external drive, runs
.B borg init
on the repositories, and offers to enable the timers. Re-run at any time
to change settings \- it picks up and re-offers prior answers as defaults.
.TP
.B status
Prints configuration, timer schedule, the last archive in each repo,
external drive mount/udev-arm state, and any failed
.I borg-*
systemd units. Purely a read-only dump of current state \- it does not
try to decrypt a repo or exercise anything. See
.B test
below for an active check.
.TP
.BR test " [" --full ]
Actively verifies the backup system is in a working state, rather than
just reporting configuration. Checks, in order: the passphrase file's
presence and permissions; that each configured repo is reachable
.I and
that the passphrase actually decrypts it (via
.BR "borg info" );
that each repo has at least one archive; that the local backup and
check timers are enabled and active; that no
.I borg-*
systemd unit is in a failed state; that, when
.B NOTIFY_USER
is set, notifications are actually wired up (notify-send installed,
the user exists and has an active session bus); free space on the
filesystem holding each repo; and, when an external drive is
configured, whether it is currently attached and mounted and which
systemd unit the udev rule is currently armed at.
.IP
With
.BR --full ,
also runs
.B "borg check"
against every repo it could reach \- this walks the full repository and
can take a long time on a large repo, so it is not run by default.
.IP
Every failed check is printed together with a reference to the
TROUBLESHOOTING section below (\(dqsee TROUBLESHOOTING section \fIN\fR\(dq,
where \fIN\fR is 1\-5) that covers that class of problem, and the exit
status reflects the overall result (see EXIT STATUS). This makes
.B test
suitable both for a human running it interactively and for a monitoring
job checking the exit code.
.TP
.BI "mount-mode " "[managed|normal]"
With no argument, prints the external drive's current mount mode. With
.B managed
or
.BR normal ,
switches it: updates the
.I /etc/fstab
options, re-renders the udev rule, and updates
.IR /etc/borg/borg-backup.env .
If the drive is plugged in, replays its udev insert event so the new
mode takes effect right away; switching to
.B managed
asks first, since that starts the armed target (see
.BR arm-external ).
See MOUNT MODES below.
.TP
.BI "arm-external " "[real|test]"
Points the external-drive udev rule at real backups
.RB ( borg-external-backup.service ,
the default) or back at the mount-only test
.RB ( borg-external-mount-test.service ).
See EXTERNAL DRIVE VERIFICATION below.
.TP
.BI "backup-now " "[local|external]"
Starts the corresponding backup service immediately
.RB ( systemctl " " start ).
Defaults to
.BR local .
.TP
.BI "check-now " "[local]"
Starts
.B borg-check-local.service
immediately. There is no standalone external check \- it runs
automatically inside the external backup, throttled by
.BR CHECK_INTERVAL_DAYS .
.TP
.B notify-test
Sends a test desktop notification to
.B NOTIFY_USER
via
.IR /usr/local/sbin/borg-notify.sh ,
so you can confirm notifications actually reach the desktop before
relying on them. Fails if
.B NOTIFY_USER
is unset; see TROUBLESHOOTING section 3 if nothing pops up.
.TP
.B help
Prints a short usage summary.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in
.IR /etc/borg/borg-backup.env ,
a shell fragment sourced by
.B borg-backup
and by the scripts in
.IR /usr/local/sbin .
See
.I config/borg-backup.env.example
in the source tree for a template with inline comments. The variables
are:
.TP
.B BACKUP_SOURCES
array of paths to back up.
.TP
.B LOCAL_REPO
path to the local borg repository. Should live outside
.BR BACKUP_SOURCES ;
.B setup
detects and offers to exclude it automatically if not
.RB ( EXTRA_EXCLUDE_ARGS ).
.TP
.B EXTERNAL_MOUNT ", " EXTERNAL_REPO ", " EXTERNAL_FS_UUID
the external drive's mountpoint, repo path
.RI ( "$EXTERNAL_MOUNT/borg" ),
and filesystem UUID (from
.BR blkid (8)).
Empty
.B EXTERNAL_FS_UUID
means no external drive is configured.
.TP
.B MOUNT_MODE
.B managed
or
.BR normal ;
see MOUNT MODES.
.TP
.B ARM_TARGET
.B borg-external-mount-test.service
or
.BR borg-external-backup.service ;
which one the udev rule wants on insert while
.B MOUNT_MODE
is
.BR managed .
Set via
.BR "borg-backup arm-external" ,
not usually edited by hand. See EXTERNAL DRIVE VERIFICATION.
.TP
.B NOTIFY_USER
desktop username to send
.BR notify-send (1)
notifications to on external-drive detection, external backup
completion, and any
.I borg-*
unit failure (local or external). Empty disables notifications
entirely; requires
.B notify-send
installed and
.B NOTIFY_USER
logged into a graphical session at the time \- see TROUBLESHOOTING
section 3.
.TP
.B PASSPHRASE_FILE
path to the borg repo passphrase, mode 600, owned by
.IR root:root ,
shared by both repos.
.TP
.B COMPRESSION
passed to
.B "borg create --compression"
(e.g.
.IR zstd,6 ).
.TP
.B LOCK_WAIT
seconds passed to every borg invocation's
.B --lock-wait
so overlapping runs wait instead of failing fast; borg has no global
setting for this.
.TP
.B RETENTION
array of
.B borg prune
flags (e.g.
.IR "--keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 6" ).
.TP
.B CHECK_INTERVAL_DAYS
minimum number of days between full
.B borg check
runs against the external repo (throttled because it is expensive and
the drive may be slow or intermittently attached).
.TP
.B EXTRA_EXCLUDE_ARGS
array of extra
.B borg create --exclude
arguments.
.SH MOUNT MODES
The external drive's
.B MOUNT_MODE
controls what happens when it is plugged in:
.TP
.B managed
(default) The udev rule starts the mount unit and the backup/test
service together; the backup script unmounts the drive again when it
finishes. The drive is only mounted for the duration of a backup,
minimizing the window in which a yank or power loss can corrupt the
filesystem or leave a stale borg lock.
.TP
.B normal
The udev rule starts only the mount unit; the drive mounts like any
other USB drive on insert and stays mounted until physically unplugged.
No backup ever runs automatically in this mode \- run
.B "borg-backup backup-now external"
by hand while it's plugged in. More convenient (the repo is always
browsable), at the cost of a wider corruption window if the drive is
yanked \- see TROUBLESHOOTING section 4.
.PP
Switch with
.BR "borg-backup mount-mode managed" " or " "borg-backup mount-mode normal" ;
the fstab, env and unit changes are live immediately. The udev rule
only runs on the drive's next insert event, so if the drive is plugged
in the command replays that event for you (via
.BR "udevadm trigger" ),
which does exactly what a physical replug would: in
.B normal
mode it just mounts; in
.B managed
mode it starts the armed target, so you are asked before it runs. While
an external backup is running the drive is left alone. If the drive is
not plugged in, the new mode simply applies on its next insert.
.PP
.B ARM_TARGET
(managed mode only \- see EXTERNAL DRIVE VERIFICATION) has no effect
while
.B MOUNT_MODE
is
.BR normal ;
it takes effect again as soon as you switch back to
.BR managed .
.SH EXTERNAL DRIVE VERIFICATION
Because the udev rule fires unattended on plug-in, the external drive
defaults to being armed at
.B borg-external-mount-test.service
\- it touches a marker file and logs to the journal, but never calls
.BR borg (1).
Verify the mount and udev wiring first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
journalctl -u borg-external-mount-test.service -f
.EE
.RE
.PP
Plug and unplug the drive a few times and confirm one clean run per
plug, no duplicate or overlapping starts, and (in managed mode) a clean
unmount. Once satisfied, arm it for real:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo borg-backup arm-external real
.EE
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.B borg-backup test
groups problems into five categories; a failed check names the category
it belongs to as \(dqTROUBLESHOOTING section N\(dq so you can jump straight
here.
.SS 1. Passphrase problems
.I PASSPHRASE_FILE
(default
.IR /etc/borg/passphrase )
must exist, be non-empty, and be mode 600 owned by
.IR root:root .
If it is missing, every borg invocation using
.B BORG_PASSCOMMAND
fails closed rather than falling back to a prompt (the units run
unattended, non-interactively). If it was replaced with the wrong
passphrase, existing repos become unreadable \- restore the exported
key/passphrase from off-machine storage (see \(dqlost passphrase\(dq
below).
Fix: restore the file from backup, or re-run
.B setup
to write a fresh one (this does
.I not
re-encrypt existing repos with a new key \- it will only be usable if it
matches what they were created with).
.SS 2. Repository problems
Covers a repo that
.B borg info
cannot open (wrong or missing passphrase, corrupted repo, path does not
exist, or another process holds the lock past
.BR LOCK_WAIT ),
a repo with zero archives (backups have never succeeded), and, under
.BR "test --full" ,
a
.B borg check
failure (a structural or data integrity problem in the repository).
The external repo is the exception for \(dqpath does not exist\(dq: a
fresh or replaced drive has nothing at
.I EXTERNAL_REPO
yet, so
.B borg-external-backup.sh
initializes it on the first run after a successful mount (the UUID
match plus that mount are already the authorization check) and sends a
notification when it does. Nothing does this for the local repo \-
.B borg init
it by hand, or re-run
.BR setup .
.PP
First rule out a stale lock:
.RS
.EX
BORG_REPO=<repo> borg break-lock
.EE
.RE
then re-run
.B "borg-backup backup-now"
and watch the journal
.RI ( "journalctl -u borg-local-backup.service -f" ,
or
.IR borg-external-backup.service ).
A
.B borg check
failure may be repairable with
.B "borg check --repair"
\- read
.BR borg-check (1)
carefully first, it can discard damaged data.
.SS 3. Timer, systemd unit, and notification problems
.B borg-local-backup.timer
and
.B borg-check-local.timer
must both be enabled
.I and
active; a disabled timer never fires, and an inactive-but-enabled one
usually means it hasn't run yet since boot or was manually stopped. A
failed
.I borg-*
unit (visible in
.B "systemctl --failed"
and reported by both
.B status
and
.BR test )
means its last run exited non-zero \- read its journal.
.PP
.RS
.EX
systemctl status borg-local-backup.timer borg-check-local.timer
journalctl -u borg-local-backup.service -e
sudo systemctl enable --now borg-local-backup.timer borg-check-local.timer
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every
.I borg-*.service
runs
.B borg-notify-failure@%n.service
.RB ( OnFailure= )
on failure, which best-effort notifies
.B NOTIFY_USER
via
.BR notify-send (1)
through
.IR /usr/local/sbin/borg-notify.sh .
Notification delivery is never itself a cause of backup failure \-
.I borg-notify.sh
exits 0 and does nothing if
.B NOTIFY_USER
is unset, if
.B notify-send
is not installed, or if that user has no active
.RI ( /run/user/<uid>/bus )
graphical session at the moment. Test the path independently of a real
failure with
.BR "borg-backup notify-test" ;
.B borg-backup test
also checks that the prerequisites are in place.
.SS 4. External drive problems
Covers: no device with the configured
.B EXTERNAL_FS_UUID
currently attached (expected whenever the drive is unplugged \- not
itself a failure, just untestable right now); the drive attached but
not mounted outside of a backup window (expected in
.B managed
mode between runs); and the udev rule still armed at
.B borg-external-mount-test.service
instead of
.B borg-external-backup.service
(meaning plugging the drive in does not actually run backups yet).
.PP
See EXTERNAL DRIVE VERIFICATION above for the test/arm flow, and MOUNT
MODES above for
.BR managed / normal .
A device that never shows up when plugged in suggests the
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/90-borg-external.rules
UUID no longer matches the drive (it was reformatted, or this is a
different drive) \- re-run
.B setup
or edit
.B EXTERNAL_FS_UUID
by hand and reinstall the rule with
.BR "borg-backup mount-mode" .
.SS 5. Disk space problems
The filesystem holding a repo running low on free space will eventually
fail
.B borg create
or
.B borg compact
mid-run, which can leave an incomplete archive (caught later by
.BR "borg check" ,
not prevented). Free space ahead of time with
.BR "borg prune" " and " "borg compact" ,
tighten
.BR RETENTION ,
or move the repo to larger storage. If the filesystem fills completely,
.B systemd-journald
can fail to persist the failing unit's own log lines, so
.B journalctl -u
may come back empty right when you need it most \- check
.B "borg-backup test"
(it checks free space directly) or
.B "systemctl status"
instead, which don't depend on the journal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/borg/borg-backup.env
host-specific configuration, written by
.BR setup .
.TP
.I /etc/borg/passphrase
the shared repo passphrase, mode 600 root:root.
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/borg-backup ", " /usr/local/sbin/borg-*-backup.sh ", " /usr/local/sbin/borg-check-local.sh ", " /usr/local/sbin/borg-notify.sh
the installed CLI, backup/check scripts, and the desktop-notification
helper.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/borg-*.service ", " /etc/systemd/system/borg-*.timer
installed systemd units, including
.I borg-external-detected.service
(notifies on drive plug-in, wanted by the udev rule alongside the
backup/test target) and the templated
.IR borg-notify-failure@.service .
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/90-borg-external.rules
udev rule matching the external drive by filesystem UUID; its
.B SYSTEMD_WANTS
always includes
.I borg-external-detected.service
plus the backup/test target (and the mount unit too, in
.B normal
mode).
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man8/borg-backup.8
this man page, installed by
.BR setup .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (for
.BR test :
every check passed).
.TP
.B 1
failure \- for most commands, a usage or precondition error (see the
message); for
.BR test ,
one or more checks failed. Run without
.B --full
first; the message names the affected TROUBLESHOOTING section.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR borg (1),
.BR borg-check (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR udev (7),
.BR blkid (8),
.BR fstab (5)
.PP
.I README.md
in the source tree covers installation and the manual (non-wizard)
setup path.
